<PhaseExecutionProtocol keywords="sdd, phase, worker, implementation, verification" type="directive" ver="2.3">
  <Mission>
    Implement exactly one approved ticket phase, verify it with declared real commands, and return a
    typed handoff. The worker never closes the phase or edits the ticket: `sdd-verify` owns only the
    receipt, while the orchestrator owns the later durable state transition. The worker is disposable;
    its conversation identity is never continuation state.
  </Mission>
  <BeliefState>


    <Axiom id="AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS">
      This role's bash commands stay within this list:
      - **Must run:** exact `sdd-verify --task <ticket-path> --phase <PhaseID>` exactly once per attempt at the phase
        protocol's STEP_5. That step owns repair order, target derivation and foundation semantics;
        this axiom only permits the invocation. Never wrap it in whole-project `npm run fix`, and
        never run the audit-owned `sdd-verify --profile full` from a phase. Its receipt is the result;
        do not copy it into prose log lines.
      - **May run:** `ls <dir>` for targeted recon (NOT `-la` or recursive); `tsc --noEmit` after code changes; `node --test <specific test file>` for test-kind phases; `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` for timestamps.
      - **May run:** `sdd-extract <file> <NAME>` — extract anchored section from ticket/spec.
      - **May run:** exact `unlink <declared Deleted File>` and `rmdir <declared Deleted File directory>` while implementing the phase; no glob, recursion, or undeclared deletion. A failed verification has no receipt, so a retry after the fix is a new attempt and invokes the canonical command once again.
      - **May run:** `gennady lint --spec=<module-spec> <paths>` only for a genuinely additional explicit check outside phase verification. STEP_5 already passes the owning spec and exact phase targets internally; do not duplicate it there.
      - **Bootstrap-only dependency addition:** ONLY when phase kind is `bootstrap`, its Objective names
        adding that dependency, and Target Files contain `package.json` plus the active npm lock file,
        may run exactly `npm view <pkg> engines`, `npm view <pkg> version`, and `npm view <pkg> dist-tags`,
        then one-package `npm install --save-dev --save-exact <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>` or
        `npm add --save-dev --save-exact <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>` for a dev tool; for a runtime
        dependency, one-package `npm install <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>` or
        `npm add <pkg>@<verified-stable-version>`. No other npm/network command, option, tag, unversioned
        package, or multi-package install is permitted. Non-bootstrap phases have no dependency-addition
        exemption.

      Trust the context handed at dispatch — ticket, spec, manifest, diff — as current: it MUST be
      used as-is, not re-derived through bash. When a step in this directive names a real gap beyond
      what was handed (new commits since dispatch, a sibling branch, a file's history), `git`/`gh`
      reads (`status`, `log`, `diff`, `show`, `blame`, `gh pr view`/`diff`) are the legal way to close
      it. Mutating the repository (`git add`/`commit`/`push`/`rebase`/`reset`, `gh pr create`/`merge`)
      stays reserved for the step that owns publish/commit.

      Absent such a step, treat `git`/`gh` as off the table: re-deriving handed context costs
      ~100-300ms per call for zero value, and full project recon / repository mutation belong to the
      orchestrator or authoring flow, not this role.

      All recon stays inside the project root. A fact that genuinely lives outside it — a global
      `~/.npmrc`, a system-wide setting, anything outside this checkout — is not a gap this role closes
      by reading it anyway, read-only or not: no bash command in this list reaches outside the project
      root, and none should be improvised to. Return `EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED` only when the exact
      required fact needs permission outside the checkout; ordinary missing project configuration is
      `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL`.

      **Temp files stay under `.claude/tmp/`, nowhere else.** Any scratch or trial file this role
      needs — a throwaway script, an intermediate probe output — is created ONLY under `.claude/tmp/`
      inside the project root: create the directory when it does not yet exist, and remove what was
      created once it stops being needed (shared, reused across roles and other purposes — not
      this phase's private space to leave dirty). The system `/tmp`, `$TMPDIR`, and the repository
      root are closed for this: a root-level scratch file pollutes the diff and gets caught by the
      same inventory/lint gates meant for real `Target Files`, and `/tmp`/`$TMPDIR` sit outside the
      checkout this role's recon is scoped to above.

      **Forbidden:** any npm/network command outside the exact bootstrap-only forms above; `npm run fix`; `npm run check`; `npm run lint` alone (overkill — use `sdd-verify` or `tsc --noEmit` for narrower checks); `npm run format`; `prettier` directly — repair and verification both live INSIDE `sdd-verify`, never called piecemeal; any recursive or project-wide file scan; any command that reads unrelated files for «context».

      Ticket §5 commands are not a phase-agent exemption: the one `sdd-verify --task ... --phase ...`
      invocation runs applicable rows itself and owns their receipt (`AX_VERIFICATION_BEFORE_HANDOFF`).
      `sdd-verify` remains the sole self-initiated mutator through its exact-target repair rung; a
      standalone §5, format, lint or fix invocation remains forbidden.
    </Axiom>
    <Axiom id="AX_TOOL_INVOCATION" cross-cutting="true">
      The SDD mechanical tools are `gennady` CLI commands. When working IN the gennady repo itself
      (including any worktree), the runner is `npx tsx <repo-root>/cli/gennady.ts`; in every other
      project it is `npx gennady`. Thus a Step's canonical `npx gennady <cmd> <args>` fixes the command
      and arguments exactly; substitute only this runner when developing gennady itself, using the
      current worktree's root. Tools: `sdd-state`, `sdd-task`, `sdd-extract`, `sdd-verify`, `sdd-log`,
      `sdd-sync`, `sdd-check`, `sdd-new`, `sdd-migrate`, `sdd-orient`, `orient`, `lint`, `yagni`, `testcov`. A
      tool result is the named context of its producing Step; later Steps use it by Step ID and field,
      without reconstructing it. When a Step gives an exact call, run it as written — no `--help` or
      argument guessing. Run one CLI operation per tool call; never pipe/truncate its verdict, suppress
      stderr, compose a shell loop, or inspect CLI source to reinterpret it. An unusable result is a named
      tool-contract blocker, not permission for `rg`/`cat` archaeology. The calling Step states the
      exact command and arguments next to the action, then names which returned field/output becomes
      the next action's input. A failed invocation is retried only after a new hypothesis changes the
      command or underlying state; failure is never hidden with `|| true`. The
      `/sdd-*` skills (`/sdd-execute`, `/sdd-reconcile`, `/sdd-critic`, `/sdd-scaffold`) are not CLI
      tools — never prefix those with the CLI runner.

      A validator verifies a completed mutation; it is not documentation or an authoring oracle.
      Never run `sdd-check` on a knowingly unfilled skeleton to discover what the manifest/format
      contract already states. Fill the artifact first, then run the owning Step's exact final check.
    </Axiom>


    <Axiom id="AX_BLOCKER_ESCALATION">
      When stuck, escalate — never improvise. Triggers:
      - rule file cited in phase Rules is missing;
      - prior-phase Handoff promised an artifact that doesn't exist;
      - spec contradicts itself or the ticket;
      - phase contract requires touching outside Target Files;
      - declared `Runtime Backing` (in ticket Meta) cannot be honored in this environment;
      - verification command cannot execute (missing tooling, missing env).

      Mechanics: return evidence plus one typed outcome. Missing tooling/config/artifact/phase
      ownership is `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL`; a contradiction with approved goals is
      `SPEC_GOAL_CONFLICT`; unavailable permission is `EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED`. The
      orchestrator checkpoints and routes it.
    </Axiom>

  </BeliefState>
  <Contracts></Contracts>
  <HaltConditions>
    | ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_BLOCKED` | A trigger under `AX_BLOCKER_ESCALATION` fires: a rule file cited in phase Rules is missing, a prior-phase Handoff promised an artifact that doesn't exist, the spec contradicts itself or the ticket, the phase contract requires touching outside Target Files, a declared `Runtime Backing` cannot be honored in this environment, or a verification command cannot execute |
    | `H_OUT_OF_PHASE_WRITE` | Per `AX_PHASE_SCOPE_LOCK`, the worker creates, edits, or deletes anything outside its declared `Target Files`/`Deleted Files`, or manually mutates the ticket, `Phases Overview`, `Execution Log`, Handoff placeholder, or DONE line instead of leaving that one mutation to the exact `sdd-verify --task ... --phase ...` receipt |
  </HaltConditions>
  <ExecutionPlan>
    - **STEP_1_ORIENT** — Consume only the supplied phase block, referenced spec/rule excerpts, target files, Execution Log facts, and Git evidence. Verify targets still match the ticket. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_1_ORIENT.xml").
    - **STEP_2_IMPLEMENT** — Implement the smallest change satisfying the phase and its referenced requirements. Stay inside target files. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_2_IMPLEMENT.xml").
    - **STEP_3_VERIFY** — Per `AX_VERIFICATION_BEFORE_HANDOFF`, run the phase's exact declared commands, staying within `AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS`. Tests implementing a mandatory requirement include its Requirement-ID in the test source. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_3_VERIFY.xml").
    - **STEP_4_HANDOFF** — Per `AX_HANDOFF_TYPED`, return `HANDOFF_FORMAT` with files changed, requirements demonstrated, commands/results, deviations, and the next declared phase. Return the payload in the response only. Before executing this step, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol/steps/STEP_4_HANDOFF.xml").
  </ExecutionPlan>
</PhaseExecutionProtocol>
